findings.body:
`RequestHandler.cookies` was a documented public property; the diff deletes it outright and rewrites the two internal call sites to `self.request.cookies`. Any application handler or third-party mixin that reads `self.cookies` (or overrides it) now gets an `AttributeError` at runtime rather than a deprecation. Keep a thin forwarding property on `RequestHandler`:

    @property
    def cookies(self):
        return self.request.cookies

The previous implementation always returned a string — anything that was not `"click"` fell through to the `key ...` return. The new `switch` has no `default` and no trailing `return`, so if an `Event` arrives at runtime with a `kind` outside the declared union (e.g. deserialised JSON, a value from a native/DOM bridge, or a future variant added elsewhere), `describe` returns `undefined` while its signature still promises `string`. Callers doing `describe(e).length` or concatenation will throw or render `"undefined"`.

If exhaustiveness is meant to be enforced only at compile time, add an explicit exhaustiveness guard that still returns a value, e.g.

```ts
default: {
  const _exhaustive: never = event;
  return `unknown event ${JSON.stringify(event)}`;
}
```

That keeps the compile-time check that motivated this refactor while preserving the old total-function behaviour.

`search_customers` composes its query with an f-string, placing `term` and `status` directly inside single-quoted SQL literals, then calls `execute(sql)` with no params. A value containing a single quote (e.g. `status="x' OR '1'='1"`, or `term="%'; DROP TABLE customers; --"`) terminates the literal and the remainder is parsed as SQL, allowing arbitrary read or modification of the database under the application's DB role.

Use the same parameterised form as `get_customer` on line 5 and let the driver bind the values, keeping the wildcards in the parameter rather than the SQL text:

```python
def search_customers(term, status):
    return execute(
        "SELECT * FROM customers WHERE name LIKE %s AND status = %s",
        (f"%{term}%", status),
    )
```

If `term` should not permit user-supplied `%`/`_` wildcards, escape those before binding.
